Unit proof: trim_from_last_delimiter's contract (empty when the
delimiter is absent; otherwise the exact prefix up to the LAST
delimiter) proven over ALL printable strings up to length 4.
KNOWNBUG: the assertions all hold, but the suite's soundness gate
trips: a derived-to-base struct VALUE typecast --
(std::__new_allocator<char>)<basic_string value>, from libstdc++'s
_Alloc_hider EBO base handling -- reaches the solver, which cannot
bit-blast struct typecasts and havocs the value ("warning: ignoring
typecast", boolbvt::conversion_failed).  Here the havocked value is a
stateless allocator, so the result is unaffected, but the pattern is
unsound in general: the front end should lower derived-to-base value
casts to base-component extraction.  g++/clang++ accept the harness
and it verifies at runtime.
